it even grosser for Melora's reaction.

1018
00:42:05.560 --> 00:42:06.900
That is so funny.

1019
00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:08.140
That is so funny.

1020
00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:12.380
And just makes me like love our show because I felt like we were just like this little

1021
00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:16.600
engine that could, this little team of like, we need two people to make slurping sounds.

1022
00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:18.120
Oh, that happened all the time.

1023
00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:18.780
Yeah, all the time.

1024
00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:20.540
You have some more stuff about Tim, though.

1025
00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:21.140
What do you want to say?

1026
00:42:21.300 --> 00:42:25.020
Well, I just asked him, you know, if he, you know, had heard of The Office and how he

1027
00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:26.020
came to get the job.

1028
00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:29.260
And he said that he was a humongous fan of the BBC version.

1029
00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:35.340
And he said that when he got the call about the show, that it was not a hit, right?

1030
00:42:35.540 --> 00:42:36.640
It was kind of early on.

1031
00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:39.680
It was still sort of like, what is this show about?

1032
00:42:39.980 --> 00:42:44.660
And like, he said in like inner comedy circles, people were like, the show is really funny.

1033
00:42:44.800 --> 00:42:48.620
So he knew it was funny, but it was like, the world didn't really know about this show

1034
00:42:48.620 --> 00:42:48.900
yet.

1035
00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:51.740
And he was kind of busy and he was like, I don't know, guys.

1036
00:42:51.840 --> 00:42:53.040
I don't know that I can do it.

1037
00:42:53.440 --> 00:42:55.840
And so he initially said no.

1038
00:42:56.080 --> 00:42:57.380
Oh, he turned it down?

1039
00:42:57.600 --> 00:42:57.920
Yes.

1040
00:42:57.920 --> 00:43:02.740
And then they called back and Tim said his theatrical manager's assistant was like, Tim,

1041
00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:04.240
this show is so hilarious.

1042
00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:06.580
And he was like, all right, all right, all right, I'll do it, I'll do it.

1043
00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:08.300
And he said he's so glad he did.

1044
00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:09.940
Of course, he had the best time with Steve.

1045
00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:13.300
And Tim is right now on a show on ABC called Schooled.

1046
00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:15.180
It's on Wednesday at 8.30 p.m.

1047
00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:15.860
So check it out.

1048
00:43:16.000 --> 00:43:16.380
Thanks, Tim.

1049
00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:20.700
So now, Angela, I think we should go back into the office and talk a little bit about Threat

1050
00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:21.660
Level Midnight.

1051
00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:22.620
Oh, yes.

1052
00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:25.460
Everyone in the office goes into the conference room.

1053
00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:28.580
They divide up the parts and they start reading Threat Level Midnight.

1054
00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:32.940
I think this is a great example of what a table read is like.

1055
00:43:33.200 --> 00:43:33.340
Yeah.

1056
00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:35.520
This is what we would do every week on the office.

1057
00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:40.420
Tuesdays or Wednesdays at lunch, we would all go into this conference room.

1058
00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:44.340
They would hand out the next week's script and we would read it out loud.

1059
00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:46.880
Yeah, and usually the director was the narrator.

1060
00:43:47.140 --> 00:43:48.880
They would read all the stage direction.

1061
00:43:49.160 --> 00:43:53.540
And on Threat Level Midnight, Jim is the narrator when you guys all read it.

1062
00:43:53.620 --> 00:43:56.040
And he casts Oscar as Golden Face.

1063
00:43:56.260 --> 00:44:00.900
But when Threat Level Midnight is actually made, Jim is Golden Face.

1064
00:44:01.060 --> 00:44:01.460
Yes.

1065
00:44:01.960 --> 00:44:02.820
Yes, that's right.

1066
00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:03.680
The little catch there.

1067
00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:06.520
One of my favorite things in this is Dwigged.

1068
00:44:06.700 --> 00:44:06.980
Mm-hmm.

1069
00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:13.080
The fact that Michael's bumbling assistant clearly used to be named Dwight.

1070
00:44:13.260 --> 00:44:19.460
So that talking head that Pam gives where she explains the typo and all of that, the first

1071
00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:23.780
time we shot this episode, that explanation did not exiSt.

1072
00:44:24.140 --> 00:44:25.800
And so that was a reshoot.

1073
00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:30.960
I actually like that talking head because it helped it make sense for me because Samuel L.

1074
00:44:31.120 --> 00:44:32.860
Chang was actually Dwigged.

1075
00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:33.760
That's right.

1076
00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:38.340
Well, here's the cast that we see in Threat Level Midnight at the table read.

1077
00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:42.200
Now, it's more fleshed out and things have changed once it's an actual movie.

1078
00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:43.660
And we see that in season seven.

1079
00:44:44.180 --> 00:44:45.740
Phyllis is Catherine Zeta-Jones.

1080
00:44:46.180 --> 00:44:47.600
Dwight is Michael Skarn.

1081
00:44:48.020 --> 00:44:49.300
Oscar is Golden Face.

1082
00:44:49.660 --> 00:44:51.080
Ryan is Samuel L.

1083
00:44:51.220 --> 00:44:51.440
Chang.

1084
00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:54.800
And that's the only people that are cast in what we see.

1085
00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:57.020
Everyone else is sort of sitting around.

1086
00:44:57.180 --> 00:44:58.660
But those are the ones that are called out.

1087
00:44:58.860 --> 00:45:00.560
Well, I had a big fan question here.

1088
00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:04.740
A lot of people asked if Threat Level Midnight was a fully written script.

1089
00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:06.920
It was written.

1090
00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:10.900
They did write quite a bit so that we would have something to work with.

1091
00:45:11.020 --> 00:45:14.600
But as you see in the show, it's an unfinished script.

1092
00:45:14.600 --> 00:45:16.420
So it was not a fully written script.

1093
00:45:16.640 --> 00:45:20.560
They also wanted to know if we read from the script or if it was improvised.

1094
00:45:21.220 --> 00:45:24.520
I was going to say that I thought we read from the script.

1095
00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:31.200
But I found an interview with Brian Baumgartner where he said we improvised it.

1096
00:45:31.360 --> 00:45:32.820
And BJ backed that up.

1097
00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:37.160
That we were sitting around for about five to ten minutes.

1098
00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:41.180
And we were just throwing out anything at all.

1099
00:45:41.300 --> 00:45:43.000
Which seems almost impossible to me.

1100
00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:45.300
But there's two people in the cast who say that's how it was.

1101
00:45:45.380 --> 00:45:46.200
I remember it differently.

1102
00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:49.380
Even Phyllis when she said, I have two things to say.

1103
00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:50.620
First thing is I love you.

1104
00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:52.020
No, no, no.

1105
00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:54.960
So I should say some of it was scripted.

1106
00:45:55.120 --> 00:45:57.940
But we did also improvise some.

1107
00:45:58.160 --> 00:45:58.400
Okay.

1108
00:45:58.540 --> 00:45:59.200
So it was both.

1109
00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:00.820
And that happened a lot when we were shooting.

1110
00:46:01.000 --> 00:46:02.380
They would leave the cameras rolling.

1111
00:46:02.780 --> 00:46:02.960
Yeah.

1112
00:46:03.020 --> 00:46:05.080
And then we would throw stuff out there.

1113
00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:07.540
And some of the improvisations stayed in.

1114
00:46:07.740 --> 00:46:08.360
I love that.

1115
00:46:08.480 --> 00:46:10.940
But you could definitely tell the beginning of the scene was scripted.

1116
00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:11.120
Yes.

1117
00:46:11.120 --> 00:46:12.140
Getting you into it.

1118
00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:12.920
Yes, that's true.

1119
00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:17.820
Back at Chili's, guys, let's have a little bit of an adult beverage time code count.

1120
00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:18.400
Oh, dear.

1121
00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:22.740
So at 7 minutes 59 seconds, Jan orders a vodka tonic.

1122
00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:24.620
We never see this vodka tonic.

1123
00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:25.840
Oh, that's right.

1124
00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:26.200
Yeah.

1125
00:46:26.520 --> 00:46:29.480
At 8 minutes 35 seconds, they all have margaritas.

